To address these limitations we present an experimentally validated optimization of GLE loading in chitosan–STTP microcapsules prepared by ionic gelation, with characterization and inhibition testing performed at Diponegoro University, Semarang. The contributions of this paper are as follows:
 - Preparation and characterization of GLE obtained by probe ultrasonication and concentrated by vacuum evaporation, with XRF and FTIR fingerprinting of the extract;
 - Systematic microencapsulation across six extract loading levels ($500$, $1000$, $2000$, $3500$, $5000$, $8000$ ppm) using controlled chitosan and STTP dosing;
 - Quantitative relations between extract loading and particle size / PDI measured by PSA, and identification of an optimal loading range $2000$--$3500$ ppm producing $315$--$390$ nm particles with $\mathrm{PDI}<0.5$;
 - Leaching tests on $1	imes1	imes1$ cm pyrite cubes and ICP-OES quantification showing significant reductions in S and Fe release for microencapsulated coatings versus uncoated and unencapsulated GLE-treated samples;
 - Discussion of mechanistic interpretations, practical deployment constraints, and suggestions for scale-up.
